{% extends 'layouts/application.njk' %}

{% block head %}
<link rel="canonical" href="https://jakeflem.com/resume/">
<meta name="robots" content="noindex">
{% endblock %}

{% block content %}

<main class="resume">
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container text-center">
      <div class="stw p-relative">
        <h1>
          <span class="resize d-block" style="padding: 0 20%;">Jake</span>
          <span class="resize d-block">Fleming</span>
        </h1>
        <h2>Product Designer</h2>
      </div>
      <p class="lead">
        I design dense technical tools for engineers and researchers, and I build a lot of them myself.
      </p>
      <p class="resume-meta">San Francisco Bay Area &middot; <a href="mailto:jcbflmng@gmail.com">jcbflmng@gmail.com</a> &middot; <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/jakeflem/">LinkedIn</a> &middot; <a href="https://github.com/jakefleming">GitHub</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <h4 class="text-center">What I'm</h4>
      <div class="stw p-relative">
        <h3 class="text-center resize">About</h3>
      </div>
      <ul>
        <li>Fifteen years designing products where the user is an engineer, a researcher, an analyst, or an investigator, and the data is the product. Five and a half of those running design at Labelbox, the data platform the big AI labs train on.</li>
        <li>My specialty is the path from "something looks off" to "here is what changed, here is how sure we are, here is what to do." Querying, comparison, drill-down, and the moment a person decides to act on what a system tells them.</li>
        <li>I make dense information calm without flattening it. Freshness, coverage, uncertainty, provenance, and rollout state are part of the design, not a tooltip.</li>
        <li>I am technically fluent in a hands-on way: I ship in the codebase (TypeScript, Swift, CSS), and I have built my own developer tools, CI integrations, and MCP servers. Engineers trust me because I have read their code.</li>
        <li>I have run a design team and I have been the only designer. I like being close to the work, and I have set direction across a product with many surfaces by building the shared system other designers use.</li>
        <li>I build what I think should exist. Those side projects have real users, real revenue, and a point of view.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <h4 class="text-center">Where I've</h4>
      <div class="stw p-relative">
        <h3 class="text-center resize">Worked</h3>
      </div>

      <div class="job">
        <div class="job-head"><span class="job-co"><a href="https://attilio.com">Attilio</a></span><span class="job-role">Founding Product Designer</span><span class="job-when">Dec 2025 &ndash; now</span></div>
        <p>First designer at a health and longevity platform. I own product design end to end and spend a large part of every week in the codebase. Built the design system that keeps a small team consistent across a client app, an advisor workspace, and an admin surface. Wired experimentation in from the start (feature flags and experiments through GrowthBook) so launches are measured, not guessed.</p>
        <p>Side effect of this job: I wanted screenshot-level regression checks on every pull request and nothing did it for native and React Native apps, so I built <a href="https://blinkdiff.com">blinkdiff</a> (below).</p>
      </div>

      <div class="job">
        <div class="job-head"><span class="job-co">Cortex</span><span class="job-role">Principal Product Designer</span><span class="job-when">May 2025 &ndash; Dec 2025</span></div>
        <p>Principal designer on an engineering intelligence platform: the catalog, scorecards, and dashboards that engineering leaders and platform teams use to understand their own systems. Pure engineering-acceleration territory: the users are engineers, the data is about their services, and the job is turning it into decisions they will act on.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="job">
        <div class="job-head"><span class="job-co">Labelbox</span><span class="job-role">VP of Design &larr; Head of Design &larr; Principal &larr; Lead</span><span class="job-when">Feb 2020 &ndash; Jun 2025</span></div>
        <p>Joined as the lead designer, built and ran the design team through Head of Design and VP, stayed hands-on throughout. Labelbox is the data labeling and model evaluation platform the large AI labs lean on, so the work was AI/ML tooling for technical users: annotation and review workflows at scale, data curation and querying, model-versus-human comparison, quality and throughput analytics, and the permissions and provenance that make a label trustworthy enough to train on. Established the design system and the interaction patterns that kept a multi-surface product coherent while it grew through the AI boom.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="job">
        <div class="job-head"><span class="job-co">Metromile</span><span class="job-role">Senior Product Designer</span><span class="job-when">Aug 2019 &ndash; Feb 2020</span></div>
        <p>Designed the AI/ML fraud-detection tools for Metromile Enterprise: an investigator working through model-flagged claims, with the evidence, the model's confidence, and the next step laid out so a human can decide quickly and defensibly.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="job">
        <div class="job-head"><span class="job-co">Tradecraft</span><span class="job-role">Product Design Track Lead</span><span class="job-when">Jul 2015 &ndash; Aug 2019</span></div>
        <p>Ran the product design program for four years: wrote the curriculum, taught it, and placed designers into growth-stage startups. Still the job that taught me the most about making a hard thing learnable.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="job">
        <div class="job-head"><span class="job-co">Earlier</span><span class="job-role">Appiphony, Piasa Games, Big Lobby</span><span class="job-when">2010 &ndash; 2015</span></div>
        <p>Enterprise product design on the Salesforce platform; co-founded an indie game studio and shipped mobile games; first startup job doing UI, branding, illustration, and animation.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <h4 class="text-center">Things I</h4>
      <div class="stw p-relative">
        <h3 class="text-center resize">Built</h3>
      </div>
      <p>These are the clearest evidence of how I think, because nobody told me to make them.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="https://blinkdiff.com">blinkdiff</a>: visual regression checks for iOS and macOS apps on every pull request. A CLI, a GitHub Action, and an approve bot. The whole product is the learning loop this role describes: instrument, launch, observe, decide, iterate, with the decision (approve or fix) made in the PR where the change lives. Deterministic by design; flakiness was the enemy and most of the engineering went into killing it.</li>
        <li><a href="https://hellbox.com">Hellbox</a>: a native macOS app for type designers that watches the source file and re-renders proofs as they work, then jumps from a glyph on the page back to the editor. Featured on the Glyphs homepage. Shipped with a live usage dashboard I built for myself: cohort views, trial-to-sale odds from historical conversion, and sound cues when someone buys.</li>
        <li>MCP servers for both of the above, so an agent can drive the tools. I care a lot about how engineers and agents will build and check software together, because I already work that way.</li>
        <li><a href="https://designercize.com">Designercize</a>: a product design challenge generator. <a href="https://www.futurefonts.xyz/jake-fleming/boxout">Boxout</a> and <a href="https://www.futurefonts.xyz/jake-fleming/goiters">Goiters</a>: typefaces. <a href="https://store.steampowered.com/app/858530/Cat_Lady/">Cat Lady</a>: a dungeon crawler on Steam.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <h4 class="text-center">Where I</h4>
      <div class="stw p-relative">
        <h3 class="text-center resize">Studied</h3>
      </div>
      <ul>
        <li>Southern Illinois University, Carbondale: B.A., Graphic Design</li>
        <li>Robert Morris University, Illinois: B.B.A., Business Administration and Management</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="text-center" style="margin-top:2rem;">
        <a class="btn btn-primary btn-lg btn-wide-mobile" href="mailto:jcbflmng@gmail.com">Get In Touch</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</main>

{% endblock %}
